{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sand-dominating-urus","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A Bedrock cutover still sends the diff to direct Anthropic, silently\n\n## Observed live, not inferred\n\nA full `rebar review-code` run with ALL THREE classes configured on Bedrock\n(`frontier = bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, `standard = bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6`)\nproduced this usage log. Every classed step went to Bedrock. One call did not:\n\n    step      op                                            model_class  model\n    base      code-review-base                              frontier     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    round_a   code-review-docs                              frontier     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    round_a   code-review-tests                             frontier     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    round_a   code-review-deletion-impact                   frontier     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    round_a   code-review-project-review-phase-boundaries    frontier     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    verify    code-review-verify                            standard     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n    coach     code-review-coach                             standard     bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n    (none)    code-novelty                                  (none)       anthropic:claude-opus-4-8   <-- LEAK\n\n(The `step`/`model_class` columns exist because of story b690; before it this row was\nindistinguishable from the others and this leak was not observable at all.)\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`code_review/workflow_ops.py:score_code_novelty` builds a hand-rolled `RunRequest` with\n`config=cfg` — the run config, whose `model` is the operator's/default `cfg.model` — and declares no\nmodel class. It is not a workflow step, so it has no `model:` declaration to honour and nothing\nroutes it through the class table. `cfg.model` defaults to `claude-opus-4-8`, a bare id that\n`infer_provider` resolves to **anthropic**, so the call goes to direct Anthropic regardless of a\nBedrock configuration.\n\n## Why this is more than a cost/consistency wart\n\nBedrock is typically chosen for data-residency, procurement, or contractual reasons. This call sends\nthe **diff under review** plus the **prior-review findings** (see the `instructions` it builds) to\ndirect Anthropic. An operator who believes they have completed a cutover has not, and nothing in the\nverdict says so.\n\nIt also directly undermines **eb6e** (\"cut the production review bot over to Bedrock\"): that story\ncannot be truthfully satisfied while this call bypasses the configuration.\n\n## The docstring claims a parity that does not hold\n\n`score_code_novelty`'s docstring says it \"Mirrors plan-review's `_score_floor_novelty` runner\nwiring\". Plan-review's novelty sub-call uses the VERIFIER config\n(`plan_review/__init__.py:164`, `config=replace(_verifier_cfg(cfg), temperature=0.0)`), so the two do\nNOT agree on config selection. Whichever way this is resolved, the docstring should stop asserting a\nparity the code does not implement.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] with all three classes configured on a non-default provider, NO call in a code-review run\n      resolves to a different provider than the configuration selects — asserted from a run's usage\n      log, which is now able to show this per call\n- [ ] the novelty sub-call's model is chosen by the same class vocabulary as the rest of the gate\n      (a class token, not a raw `cfg` inheritance), and the choice is stated in the code with its\n      reason rather than left implicit\n- [ ] the choice is consistent with plan-review's novelty sub-call, or the difference is documented\n      as deliberate at both sites — the current docstring asserts parity that does not exist\n- [ ] a test fails if a hand-built sub-call in this gate inherits `cfg.model` while a class table is\n      configured (the general defect, so a FUTURE hand-built sub-call cannot silently reintroduce it)\n- [ ] the fail-safe behaviour is preserved: a broken novelty signal must still degrade to \"keep more\n      findings\", never to a wrong drop\n\n## Related\n\nSibling to `03b0` (a configured Bedrock provider is dropped for colon-bearing model ids). Both are\nways the provider configuration fails to reach the metal; this one is silent and succeeds, which\nmakes it the more dangerous of the two. Found while gathering 3768's non-default-provider evidence.","id":"afeb-bc04-3265-4747","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"code-novelty sub-call escapes the class config and goes to direct Anthropic even in a full Bedrock run"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785621497737276001,"uuid":"ab165411-dd79-42a4-a376-3880f4b8a609"}